- KLA — inspection and process control

- Tokyo Electron — multiple wafer-processing steps

TSMC integrates these tools, its own process know-how, materials, software, and thousands of manufacturing steps into a working process node.

This is why buying ASML machines does not mean you can simply recreate TSMC.

Why moving to a new node is difficult

Imagine trying to mass-produce an object containing tens of billions of microscopic components where tiny defects can ruin the product.

A new node requires years of R&D before high-volume production becomes reliable.

One critical concept is yield.

Yield = the percentage of manufactured chips that work well enough to sell.

Suppose a wafer contains 100 potential chips.

- If 90 work, yield is roughly 90%.

- If only 40 work, the economics are terrible because much of the expensive wafer processing was wasted.

Early in a new node’s life, yields are usually worse. Over time, engineers identify defects and improve the manufacturing process.

The economic implication

This creates a major barrier to entry.

A competitor does not merely need billions of dollars of equipment. It needs years of process knowledge to make that equipment produce advanced chips at commercially attractive yields.

This is one reason TSMC’s manufacturing know-how itself is a moat.

The equipment vendors own critical technologies, but TSMC knows how to combine thousands of process steps into reliable mass production.

Why nodes are increasingly expensive

Each generation becomes harder to develop.

Leading-edge fabs can require tens of billions of dollars of investment. Individual EUV tools cost hundreds of millions of dollars, and a fab needs enormous amounts of additional equipment, clean-room infrastructure, utilities, materials, and engineering labor.

That creates an important industry dynamic:

The cost of competing at the leading edge keeps rising, while the number of companies capable of doing it has fallen.

Historically many semiconductor companies manufactured leading-edge chips. Today only a small handful can credibly compete at the frontier.

Investment lens

Do not automatically assume “smaller node = better investment.”

The more useful question is:

Who captures the economics created by increasing manufacturing complexity?

Several layers can benefit:

- ASML sells increasingly critical lithography equipment.

- KLA / Lam / Applied Materials / Tokyo Electron sell other increasingly sophisticated tools.

- TSMC monetizes scarce leading-edge manufacturing capacity.

- NVIDIA / Broadcom / other designers use advanced manufacturing to build higher-value chips.

A major theme of this curriculum will be determining which layer has the strongest combination of scarcity, pricing power, growth and defensibility.

Remember these 5 things

- 3nm does not mean every feature is literally 3nm. It is the name of a manufacturing generation.

- Newer nodes generally improve transistor density and/or performance and efficiency.

- TSMC, Samsung and Intel develop advanced manufacturing processes; NVIDIA designs chips rather than manufacturing them in leading-edge fabs.

- Yield measures how much of production is actually usable and is crucial to foundry economics.

- Owning advanced equipment is not enough. The manufacturing recipe and process knowledge are major competitive advantages.

Quick check

If Intel bought exactly the same ASML EUV machines as TSMC, would Intel automatically be able to manufacture chips with the same yield and economics as TSMC?

No. The machines are essential inputs, but process integration, recipes, engineering experience and accumulated manufacturing know-how matter enormously.

