    body: `Yesterday we covered what a process node means. Today we move one step further down the physical stack: before TSMC can print a single transistor, it needs an extraordinarily pure, flat piece of silicon.

1. What is a wafer?

A silicon wafer is the thin, circular disk on which hundreds of chips are manufactured at the same time.

Think of it like a giant sheet of cookie dough. The fab performs hundreds to thousands of process steps across the entire sheet, then at the end cuts it into individual chips — the cookies.

For leading-edge logic, the standard wafer is about 300 mm (12 inches) in diameter. A wafer is only the starting substrate; its value rises enormously as fabrication steps are added.

2. Why silicon?

Silicon is a semiconductor: its ability to conduct electricity can be precisely controlled.

That matters because a transistor is fundamentally an electrical switch. Engineers need a material that can be manipulated so the switch reliably turns current on and off billions of times per second.

Silicon also has several practical advantages: it is abundant, the industry has spent decades learning how to manufacture with it, and it forms a useful insulating oxide. That accumulated manufacturing ecosystem is a major reason silicon remains dominant.

3. How do you make a wafer?

The simplified chain is:

Quartz → ultra-pure silicon → silicon crystal → sliced wafer → polished wafer → semiconductor fab